data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Confirming Dwork et al.**\n\nI'm now confirming the citation for Dwork et al. (2015), ensuring the precise title and authors for accurate referencing. I'm also considering a permutation test on the holdout set, randomly shuffling labels to establish a baseline of chance performance. My confidence level for this is very high, around 0.90. Finally, I've decided to consult the holdout set only once, at the very end.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 2950,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1820,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Evaluation Strategy**\n\nI'm now completely focused on the holdout set evaluation strategy. I will consult it only once, after all cycles are complete and the detectors are finalized. I'm carefully formulating a falsifiable statement and a corresponding measure to quantify the impact of multiple consultations on p-value calibration under optional stopping. I'm recalling relevant literature on optional stopping and multiple testing, including Armitage, McPherson, Rowe (1969) which is very good.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 3218,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2088,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Confirming Relevant Citations**\n\nI'm now cross-referencing Dwork et al. (2015) for Q2 (adaptive cycles) and Koh et al. (2021) for Q1 (OOD scope), confirming their suitability. I'm also preparing for the *Contrôle objectif*, which will involve cryptographic timestamping (SHA-256 hash) for code and model weights. I have a high degree of certainty (0.85) about this approach, conditional on adequate sample size within differential privacy budgets. I'm moving on to the final part, Q3, which relates to contamination/leakage to authors.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 3521,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2391,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Exposure Scenarios**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining the precise fraction of examples exposed and ensuring traceability of that exposure, including specific case identifiers and their relation to architectural freezing. I'm clarifying how to split the holdout set, declassifying parts as internal development if cases are identifiable and partial, keeping unseen parts if they retain sufficient statistical power. If exposure is global or untraceable, I will reclassify the entire set as internal and create a fresh one for external scope.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 3820,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2690,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Potential Leakage**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining *Contamination*: *From Memorization to Exploitation* and referencing Recht et al. (2019) is super solid and accurate. I'm also focused on the automated audit: cross-referencing system access logs with a performance gap test between the exposed partition and a twin partition generated after freezing. My confidence is now medium-high (0.80).\n\nI am also assessing Q4, and the shift from an absolute performance threshold to two relational tests — (i) a gap from a published law and (ii) a marginal gain of the internal state on the text. This doesn't change the embargo on using the set for stopping (Q1-Q2) or managing leaks (Q3), but changes the statistical usage and gender sensitivity.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 4078,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2948,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

