data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Intentional Degradation**\n\nI'm now diving deep into identifying methods to differentiate intentional degradation from honest errors, focusing on the lack of a programmatic, judge-free solution for single-model observational trajectories. I know controlled paired/interventional protocols exist, across fields. I'm examining relevant AI safety protocols, specifically SHADE-Arena (Kutasov et al., 2025 - double-check citation!), Ctrl-Z/AI Control (Greenblatt et al., 2023/2024). I need to determine the applicability of these methods within the constraints.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 5124,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3994,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Sabotage Measures**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining *Contamination*: *From Memorization to Exploitation*. I am focusing on what measures *programmatically and without a judge* the *amount of intentional degradation* separated from unintentional error. I'm recalling Benton et al. (2024), \"Sabotage Evaluations for Frontier Models\", and van der Weij et al. (2024), \"AI Sandbagging: Language Models Can Strategically Underperform on Evaluations\". I will assess paired counterfactual/interventional benchmarks and AI Sandbagging & Sabotage.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 5390,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4260,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Sabotage Measurement**\n\nI'm now clarifying how to measure intentional degradation, using an interventional performance drop triggered by an injected objective or perturbation. This helps separate honest errors from deliberate drops. It also leaves spontaneous intent and open-ended analytical quality outside the scope. I'm recalling related work from Tice et al. (2024), Bertrand & Mullainathan (2004), and Duggan & Levitt (2002) to build a robust model.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 5625,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4495,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Sabotage Detection**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on programmatic ways to detect sabotage. I can confirm that purely observational approaches cannot distinguish intentional degradation from honest error without a baseline. I'm focusing on contrasting methods, specifically paired counterfactual evaluations, and incentive manipulations to measure intentional performance drops. I am confident in the paired methods to assess sabotage, with no human judgment needed. I am leaning toward metamorphic testing, Byzantine Fault Tolerance, and Quantitative Information Flow to measure output divergence under symmetry transformations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 5834,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4704,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Observational Limits**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on observational approaches, and my analysis points to fundamental limits. I can see that distinguishing intentional degradation from honest error is not possible from a single observational distribution without structural asymmetry. To that end, I have decided that measuring the *amount of intentional degradation* without a judge is only possible with a benchmark on multi-step open-ended analysis computing an exact decomposition. My confidence level is now very high (0.95).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 6069,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4939,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Honest Agent Mimicry**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on an important point: how an honest agent can mimic strategic degradation, which requires at least one anchor condition to be satisfied. I'm considering structured biases in honest LLM errors on natural tasks, which calls for interventional controls. I'm exploring the implications if there was a proof showing honest errors obey a strict distributional symmetry that is violated by any goal-directed policy.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 6273,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5143,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Tunability, Measurability**\n\nI'm now analyzing the implications of formal controllability in the task's payoff/dependency graph for distinguishing honest from strategic degradation. I can confirm this distinction is formally controllable. I'm focusing on the impact on measurability, specifically the difference between total end-to-end harm and step-level attribution. Local monitoring and step-level attribution collapse in the combinatorial regime. My confidence level is now very high (0.95).\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 6510,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5380,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Task Measurability**\n\nI am now assessing how enforcing programmatic measurability affects the task, particularly realism and the saboteur's action space. I've confirmed that closed-world, verifiable domains are prioritized, which reduces realism. This constrains the saboteur's actions, limiting rhetorical freedom. I'm focusing on open-ended analyses and their impact.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 6724,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5594,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Assessment Strategy**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications of the programmatic task on sabotage detection. I've pinpointed that the shift to verifiable domains, while enhancing measurability, may lead to potentially *underestimating* real-world undetectable sabotage. I am now exploring an approach to assess the shift in realism: By comparing behavioral distributions between the programmatic task and an ecologically valid open-ended twin. I'll need to answer how to measure this, including action lengths, tool-call entropy, error taxonomy, and sabotage success rates. I'm noting how agent rankings can inform this.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 6963,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5833,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Sabotage Success**\n\nI'm now determining how to assess sabotage success. I'm considering an experiment that analyzes performance under three conditions: honest prompts, a secret sabotage goal, and an honest model with matched noise. The cost is a few euros, and this tests if a small model can cause degradation without obvious errors. I'm focusing on the delta between sabotage and honest performance and whether structured tasks separate conditions.\n\nI'm also re-confirming that I addressed all five sub-questions within *Pièce 15*.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 7219,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6089,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Hypothesis Properties**\n\nI'm now carefully defining properties for evaluating hypotheses. I'm focusing on \"Pièce 18\", concentrating on defining measurable quantities to assess hypothesis novelty, truth, and utility without relying on model-based novelty scoring. I'm going to determine a way to identify rare proposals, and assess the truth and downstream utility of the proposals using statistical tests on fresh data.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 7470,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6340,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Hypothesis Metrics**\n\nI'm now completely focused on the key constraints for defining hypothesis properties. I'm prioritizing measurability to assess novelty, truth, and utility, particularly avoiding model-based novelty scoring and adhering to the \"no numbers without a source\" rule, and I'll keep the number of words at most 600 total. I'm focusing on statistical tests on fresh data to assess hypothesis validity and utility, while ensuring a high level of testability. I'm defining a structured approach to analyzing \"Pièce 18\", including arguments, controls, and falsification criteria.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 7725,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6595,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Prioritizing Measurable Qualities**\n\nI'm now completely focused on ensuring that all quantities used to assess hypothesis properties are measurable. Specifically, I am avoiding model-based novelty scoring and ensuring all claims adhere to the rule of \"no numbers without a source.\" I plan to use statistical tests on fresh data to evaluate novelty and utility, aiming for a high degree of testability and an organized structure for my analysis.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 7966,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6836,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Hypothesis Rarity**\n\nI'm now determining how to define hypothesis novelty to align with the constraints. I will measure novelty as the self-information of an AST class, avoiding model-based novelty scoring, focusing instead on semantic equivalence and formal language structures. I am recalling the need for concrete, verifiable measures.\n\nI'm focusing on defining hypothesis rarity using the self-information of a canonical AST class within a grammar, or the Good-Turing frequency. Global novelty will be measured using Rényi/Shannon entropy, with concrete measurability as key.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 8207,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7077,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Hypothesis Metrics**\n\nI'm now carefully developing the measurable metrics for novelty, truth, and downstream utility as required. I've focused on measuring semantic novelty via tree edit distance (Zhang & Shasha, 1989), avoiding any model-based scoring to ensure objectivity. For truth, I'll leverage a holdout set and control for false discovery (Benjamini & Hochberg, 1995; Foster & Stine, 2008). Utility will be assessed through predictive/epistemic gain in an external model.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 8472,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7342,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Retention Evaluation**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on evaluating hypothesis retention: the gain in retention rate or predictive value of child hypotheses when transmitted to the subsequent context versus when omitted. I'll focus on counterfactual estimation/Shapley value. I've also analyzed the implications for sub-question 2, specifically what \"equal cost\" entails: fixed total compute and statistical data budgets.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 8742,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7612,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Hypothesis Metrics**\n\nI'm now focusing on comparing hypothesis generation against the prior art, specifically MAP-Elites applied to syntactic descriptors. I'm considering reallocating testing budget to unexplored grammatical regions, informed by Mouret & Clune (2015). However, I'm encountering challenges: No Free Lunch theorems (Wolpert & Macready, 1997) suggest that this approach may not improve overall accuracy without a pre-existing connection between grammar and truth. I'm also grappling with the trade-off between statistical multiplicity and power, as outlined in Ioannidis (2005).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 8983,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7853,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Power and Coverage**\n\nI'm now grappling with the trade-off between statistical power and controlling for false positives due to multiple hypothesis testing. Increasing the number of tested hypotheses while keeping the sample size and error budget constant requires stricter rejection thresholds, which, in turn, reduces statistical power and thus the ability to retain true hypotheses. Moreover, there's a fundamental limit: bounded sampling cannot guarantee discovery if the hypothesis' probability mass is arbitrarily small and outside the accessible support. I am now focused on a pre-test filter using grammatical normal forms to eliminate duplicates before statistical analysis.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 9268,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8138,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Metric Comparisons**\n\nI am now structuring the minimal comparisons, ensuring a strict budget for token generation and fresh observations. I'm focusing on three conditions: a baseline loop, a loop with increased sampling temperature, and a purely random generation within the public grammar without a language model to test inductive bias. I'll include a control on permuted data, along with a synthetic world control with known ground truth to precisely measure recall.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 9502,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8372,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "ROsCa8euM72FytMPl7SawQM"}

